\title{The Constraint-Satisfaction Threshold of Sudoku:\\
A Sharp Phase Transition Across Order, Enumeration, and Colouring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Sudoku is the archetypal \emph{constraint-satisfaction problem} (CSP): every row,
column, and box imposes a single combinatorial demand, the \emph{AllDifferent}
constraint, requiring a block of $m$ cells to receive pairwise distinct symbols
from an alphabet of size $k$. We isolate this atomic constraint and prove that its
satisfiability undergoes a \textbf{sharp phase transition} as the block size $m$
crosses the alphabet size $k$: the constraint is satisfiable if and only if
$m \le k$, and the transition occurs at the single critical value $m_c(k) = k+1$.
We then show that this one threshold is simultaneously an \textbf{order-theoretic}
phenomenon (the satisfiable region is exactly the down-set $[0,k]$, forcing a single
jump), an \textbf{enumerative / statistical-mechanical} phenomenon (the partition
function counting proper assignments is the falling factorial $k^{\underline{m}}$,
strictly positive in the satisfiable phase and identically zero beyond criticality),
and a \textbf{graph-theoretic} phenomenon (an AllDifferent block is the complete
graph $K_m$, so satisfiability equals $k$-colourability). For an order-$n$ Sudoku the
grid is $n^2 \times n^2$ and every line contains exactly $n^2$ cells drawn from $n^2$
symbols: the puzzle sits precisely at criticality ($m = k = n^2$), which we argue is
the structural reason Sudoku is a hard, critically-constrained problem. Finally we
exhibit an explicit simultaneous solution of the row-and-column relaxation---the
cyclic Latin square $L(i,j) = i+j$ over $\Z/N\Z$---and note that it provably fails the
box constraints, isolating the boxes as genuinely independent demands.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} constraint satisfaction, AllDifferent constraint, phase
transition, falling factorial, partition function, Latin square, graph colouring,
Sudoku, pigeonhole principle.

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{constraint-satisfaction problem} (CSP) asks whether variables can be assigned
values from finite domains so that a family of constraints is jointly satisfied. CSPs
are the common substrate of graph colouring, scheduling, Boolean satisfiability, and
countless industrial optimisation problems. A recurring theme across these domains is
the existence of \emph{phase transitions}: as a control parameter crosses a critical
value, the probability that a random instance is satisfiable drops abruptly from near
$1$ to near $0$, and the hardest instances congregate at the boundary.

Sudoku is a finite, deterministic member of this family. An order-$n$ Sudoku is played
on an $n^2 \times n^2$ grid partitioned into $n^2$ boxes of size $n \times n$; the
classic puzzle is order $n = 3$. The rules decompose into a single repeated demand: each
row, column, and box must contain each of the $n^2$ symbols exactly once---equivalently,
its cells must be pairwise distinct. This atomic demand is the \textbf{AllDifferent}
constraint.

This paper isolates the AllDifferent atom, establishes that it exhibits a \emph{sharp}
satisfiability threshold, and demonstrates that the identical threshold surfaces in three
distinct mathematical languages. We then specialise to Sudoku, showing that the grid is
engineered to sit exactly at criticality, and we exhibit an explicit group-theoretic
witness for the row-and-column relaxation.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $m$ denotes the number of cells in a block (``demands'') and $k$ the size of
the symbol alphabet (``resources''). We write $[m] = \{0, 1, \ldots, m-1\}$ for the index
set of a block, realised as the finite set of size $m$.

\begin{definition}[AllDifferent satisfiability]
A block of $m$ cells is \emph{satisfiable} over an alphabet of $k$ symbols, written
$\SAT(m,k)$, if there exists an injective assignment $f : [m] \to [k]$:
\[ \SAT(m,k) \iff \exists\, f : [m] \to [k],\ f \text{ injective}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Critical cell count]
For a fixed alphabet size $k$, the \emph{critical cell count} is $m_c(k) = k+1$, the
smallest block size at which satisfiability fails.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partition function]
The \emph{partition function} of the AllDifferent atom is the number of proper
(injective) assignments,
\[ Z(m,k) = k^{\underline{m}} = k\,(k-1)\cdots(k-m+1) = \frac{k!}{(k-m)!}\quad (m \le k), \]
the falling factorial, with $Z(m,k) = 0$ when $m > k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Constraint density]
For $k > 0$, the \emph{constraint density} of a block is $\rho(m,k) = m/k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cyclic Latin square]
Over the cyclic group $\Z/N\Z$ the \emph{cyclic Latin square} is the filling
$L(i,j) = i + j \pmod N$.
\end{definition}

\section{The pigeonhole equivalence}

Every result in this paper funnels through one equivalence.

\begin{theorem}[Pigeonhole equivalence]\label{thm:pigeon}
$\SAT(m,k) \iff m \le k.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) An injection $f : [m] \to [k]$ forces $|[m]| \le |[k]|$, i.e.\ $m \le k$,
by the counting bound for injective maps between finite sets. ($\Leftarrow$) If $m \le k$,
the inclusion $i \mapsto i$ (each index of $[m]$ lies in $[k]$ since $i < m \le k$) is
injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unsatisfiability]
$\neg\,\SAT(m,k) \iff k < m.$
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Antitone in cells]
If $m' \le m$ and $\SAT(m,k)$, then $\SAT(m',k)$: removing cells cannot destroy
satisfiability.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Monotone in symbols]
If $k \le k'$ and $\SAT(m,k)$, then $\SAT(m,k')$: adding symbols cannot destroy
satisfiability.
\end{corollary}

\section{The sharp phase transition}

\begin{theorem}[Subcritical phase]
If $m < m_c(k) = k+1$, then $\SAT(m,k)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$m < k+1$ means $m \le k$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Supercritical phase]
If $m \ge m_c(k)$, then $\neg\,\SAT(m,k)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$m \ge k+1$ means $k < m$; apply the unsatisfiability corollary.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Down-set structure]
The satisfiable region is exactly the integer interval
$\{m : \SAT(m,k)\} = \{0,1,\ldots,k\} = [0,k]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}: $\SAT(m,k)$ iff $m \le k$ iff $m \in [0,k]$.
\end{proof}

Because the feasible set is a down-set with a hard ceiling, the transition is a single
jump; this is the order-theoretic content of ``sharpness''.

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the critical value]
Suppose $t$ separates a uniformly satisfiable phase from an unsatisfiable point: for all
$m < t$ we have $\SAT(m,k)$, and $\neg\,\SAT(t,k)$. Then $t = m_c(k) = k+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $\neg\,\SAT(t,k)$ we get $k < t$, so $t \ge k+1$. If $t > k+1$ then $k+1 < t$, whence
$\SAT(k+1,k)$ by hypothesis, i.e.\ $k+1 \le k$, a contradiction. Hence $t = k+1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The partition function}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity detects the phase]\label{thm:pos}
$Z(m,k) > 0 \iff m \le k \iff \SAT(m,k).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The falling factorial $k^{\underline m} = \prod_{i=0}^{m-1}(k-i)$ is a product of $m$
integer factors, all strictly positive precisely when $k-(m-1) \ge 1$, i.e.\ $m \le k$;
otherwise one factor is $0$. Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collapse above threshold]
$Z(m,k) = 0 \iff k < m.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:pos}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Value at criticality]
$Z(k,k) = k!.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$k^{\underline{k}} = k(k-1)\cdots 1 = k!$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One step past criticality]
$Z(k+1,k) = 0.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The product $k^{\underline{k+1}}$ includes the factor $k-k = 0$.
\end{proof}

The partition function decreases from $k!$ (a fully packed but solvable block) to exactly
$0$ as $m$ steps from $k$ to $k+1$. For a nine-cell Sudoku line, $Z(9,9) = 9! = 362\,880$
and $Z(10,9) = 0$.

\section{Density and the critical density $1$}

\begin{theorem}[Critical density]
For $k > 0$, $\SAT(m,k) \iff \rho(m,k) = m/k \le 1.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}, $\SAT(m,k) \iff m \le k$. Since $k > 0$, dividing by $k$
gives $m/k \le 1$, reversibly.
\end{proof}

The critical density is therefore exactly $1$: one demand per resource.

\section{Specialisation to Sudoku: the grid sits at criticality}

\begin{theorem}[Sudoku lines are critically satisfiable]
For every order $n$, an $n^2 \times n^2$ Sudoku line holding $n^2$ cells over $n^2$
symbols is satisfiable: $\SAT(n^2, n^2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$n^2 \le n^2$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}. Here $m = k = n^2$, the largest satisfiable
value.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One clue too many is fatal]
For every order $n$, $\neg\,\SAT(n^2 + 1,\ n^2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$n^2 < n^2+1$; apply the unsatisfiability corollary.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forced-collision certificate]
If $k < m$, then every assignment $f : [m] \to [k]$ repeats a symbol: there exist distinct
$i, j$ with $f(i) = f(j)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $|[k]| = k < m = |[m]|$, no map $[m] \to [k]$ can be injective, so some two distinct
indices collide.
\end{proof}

For classic $9\times 9$ Sudoku ($n = 3$), every line is $\SAT(9,9)$ but
$\neg\,\SAT(10,9)$: solvable, with zero slack.

\section{Graph-theoretic bridge: AllDifferent equals colouring $K_m$}

Model an AllDifferent block as a graph with one vertex per cell and an edge between every
pair of cells. This is the complete graph $K_m$; a proper $k$-colouring is exactly an
injective symbol assignment.

\begin{theorem}[CSP $\Leftrightarrow$ colouring]\label{thm:col}
$K_m$ is $k$-colourable if and only if $\SAT(m,k)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) A proper $k$-colouring assigns distinct colours to any two distinct
(adjacent) vertices, hence is injective. ($\Leftarrow$) An injective $f : [m] \to [k]$ is a
proper colouring.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chromatic threshold]
$K_m$ is $k$-colourable $\iff m \le k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:col} with Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon}; equivalently
$\chrom(K_m) = m$.
\end{proof}

\section{An explicit simultaneous solution: the cyclic Latin square}

\begin{theorem}[Cyclic Latin square]
For every $N \ge 1$, the filling $L(i,j) = i + j$ over $\Z/N\Z$ makes every row and every
column a bijection.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $i$. The map $j \mapsto i + j$ is injective by cancellation in the group, hence
bijective on a finite set. The column claim is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Boxes are independent]
The cyclic square satisfies all rows and columns but \emph{not} the box constraints (e.g.\
for $n = 3$ its top-left box repeats values along anti-diagonals). Hence the box demands
are genuinely new constraints, not consequences of the line demands: full Sudoku is
strictly harder than its Latin-square relaxation.
\end{remark}

\section{Criticality, computational hardness, and the ``P vs NP of Sudoku''}

The generalisation from the fixed $9 \times 9$ board to arbitrary order $n$ connects this
combinatorial threshold to computational complexity. Deciding whether a partially filled
order-$n$ Sudoku can be completed is NP-complete, and its difficulty is not evenly spread:
the hardest instances of many NP-complete problems cluster at a \emph{critical} setting of
a natural control parameter, where solutions are neither abundant nor obviously absent. The
results above give an exactly-solvable account of why Sudoku is positioned at such a point.

As the demand count $m$ varies against the resource count $k$, a constraint block passes
through three regimes: \emph{subcritical} ($m < k$), where the partition function
$Z(m,k) = k^{\underline m}$ is large and every partial assignment extends greedily;
\emph{critical} ($m = k$), where $Z = k!$ but the block is saturated, so a single forced
value removes an entire symbol and, by the down-set structure, tightens neighbouring lines
(maximal propagation); and \emph{supercritical} ($m > k$), where $Z = 0$ and infeasibility
is certain, witnessed by the forced-collision certificate.

Sudoku places every one of its $3n^2$ lines at the critical value $m = k = n^2$
simultaneously, and the three constraint families interlock so that a forced symbol
propagates through shared cells. This is the mechanism behind the familiar solving
experience: chains of forced deductions punctuated by genuine branches whose errors
surface only after propagating deep into the grid. The AllDifferent atom is thus the
exactly-solvable base case of the hardness story, pinning down the threshold location
($m = k$), its width (a single cell), and its order parameter (the falling factorial); the
remaining difficulty lives in the interaction of many critical atoms in a random ensemble
(Section~\ref{sec:future}).

\section{Discussion}

A single pigeonhole inequality $m \le k$ controls three superficially unrelated
structures: order theory sees a down-set with a hard ceiling; enumerative combinatorics /
statistical mechanics sees a partition function (falling factorial) that vanishes exactly
at the boundary; graph theory sees the chromatic number of a complete graph. Their
agreement is a consequence of each being a reformulation of the same comparison.

The specialisation $m = k = n^2$ places every line of an order-$n$ Sudoku at the last
satisfiable value---the critical point $\rho = 1$. Constraints are maximally tight while
still admitting solutions, so solutions are rigid and a single misstep propagates to a
contradiction, with an explicit certificate. This is the structural source of the puzzle's
difficulty. The box family is not implied by the line families, so the residual hardness of
Sudoku is concentrated in the interaction of the box constraints with rows and columns.

\section{Future work}\label{sec:future}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{A sharp clue-density threshold for random puzzles.} Reveal each cell of an
$n^2 \times n^2$ grid independently with probability $p$, consistently filled. Conjecture:
there is a critical clue density $p_c(n)$ below which a completion exists with probability
$\to 1$ and above which $\to 0$, with a shrinking transition window. Satisfiability is a
monotone event in the revealed clues, so the boundary is a single up-set edge.
\item \textbf{Boxes strictly lower the critical density.} Conjecture: the full Sudoku CSP
has a strictly smaller critical clue density than its Latin-square relaxation, with a gap
bounded below uniformly in $n$.
\item \textbf{The vanishing partition function as an analytic order parameter.} Conjecture:
the falling-factorial partition function admits an analytic continuation in the alphabet
size whose real zeros accumulate exactly at the critical demand count.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The AllDifferent constraint has a sharp satisfiability threshold at the balance point
$m = k$, with unique critical value $m_c(k) = k+1$ and critical density $1$. This single
threshold is at once order-theoretic (down-set $[0,k]$), enumerative /
statistical-mechanical (falling-factorial partition function, zero beyond threshold), and
chromatic ($k$-colourability of $K_m$). An order-$n$ Sudoku sits exactly on this boundary
because each line satisfies $m = k = n^2$, the structural source of its difficulty; and
while the row-and-column relaxation has the explicit cyclic solution $L(i,j) = i+j$, the box
constraints are provably independent, locating the residual hardness of full Sudoku
precisely.

